    "datePosted": "2026-11-09T06:12:40.000Z",
    "description": "Organization Profile Mujejeguwa-Loka Women Development Association (MLWDA) is a pioneering, women-led Ethiopian charity legally registered with the Ministry of Justice in 2002. Founded in 1996, MLWDA began as a grassroots initiative to address the critical health needs of Gumuz women in Benishangul-Gumuz Regional State, challenging harmful cultural practices such as giving birth in forests. Today, MLWDA has grown into a leading non-religious and non-political nonprofit organization, working to advance women’s rights, gender equality, and eliminate gender-based violence (GBV), particularly in the Benishangul-Gumuz and Amhara regions. For nearly three decades, MLWDA has worked to create a safe and empowering environment for women and girls—free from violence and discrimination—through community-led programs, advocacy, legal reforms, and sustainable development initiatives. The organization operates with a team of 67 professionals, including social workers, nurses, counselors, and program coordinators, making an impact from national to grassroots levels. Organization: Mujejeguwa Loka Women Development Association (MLWDA) Position: Project Coordinator Location: Assosa, Benishangul-Gumuz Region with frequent travel to field areas Technical Supervisor: Program Coordinator Administrative Supervisor of the Position: Project Coordinator Deadline: 13 November 2026 Job summary The SRHR Coordinator will oversee the day-to-day implementation, monitoring, and reporting of the project titled 'Improving Access to Reproductive Health Information and Services to Women and Girls in Benishangul Gumuz 2026/27'. The primary focus is to improve awareness, understanding, and knowledge among women and girls regarding gender equality and Sexual and Reproductive Health and Rights (SRHR), including the prevention of unplanned pregnancy, unsafe abortion, and Sexual and Gender-Based Violence (SGBV). Key responsibilities include: Coordinate and execute gender-responsive Comprehensive Abortion Care (CAC), Comprehensive Contraception (CC), and SGBV training and orientations for Community Volunteers, Health Extension Workers (HEWs), and School Club Leaders. Strengthen school clubs and facilitate school peer-to-peer SRHR self-learning sessions, giving priority to schools with high female dropout rates due to gold mining migrations. Conduct monthly community outreach and weekly dialogue sessions on reproductive health issues, the Revised Abortion Law, and conditions for termination of pregnancy to establish solid referral linkages. Develop, adapt, and systematically distribute Information, Education, and Communication (IEC) materials, including leaflets and posters. Organize and manage mass awareness campaigns at public gatherings, as well as media review meetings and training sessions with regional TV/Radio journalists. Support regional GBV Response Networks and maintain close multi-sectoral coordination with key stakeholders (BoFED, Regional Health Bureau, BoWSA). Supervise field monitoring visits and bi-semi-annual joint project evaluations with government stakeholders to track activity budgets and indicators.",
